# frozen_string_literal: true

module Ruby
  module Nes
    module Frontends
      # Turns a frame buffer into RGB: 3 bytes per pixel, red, green and blue, row by row, as PNG images and RGB24
      # textures take them (plan 16, decision 4). Off the hot path: once per frame presented.
      #
      # The colours are NESdev's 2C02G_U_wiki.pal (https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/File:2C02G_U_wiki.pal): "A palette
      # generated by Pally v0.23.0" (pally.py --skip-plot -cld -phd 4 -e -blp 7.5), generator "licensed under MIT-0"; the
      # wiki's content "is considered public domain" (https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/Nesdev_wiki:General_disclaimer). Its
      # 1536 bytes hold "each palette value when colour (de-)emphasis bits are applied" (https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/.pal):
      # 64 colours for each of the 8 emphasis combinations, taken from the generator's copy of the same file
      # (https://github.com/Gumball2415/pally, docs/NESDev) because the wiki refuses scripted downloads. NESdev does not
      # give the order of the blocks; the data does (block 1 is tinted red, 2 green, 4 blue, 7 darkened grey), and it is
      # the order of a pixel's emphasis bits 6-8 (red, green, blue on NTSC, https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/Colour_emphasis),
      # so a pixel (docs/architecture.md, Frame buffer contract) indexes the table directly. One block per paragraph;
      # red, green and blue of each entry as hexadecimal.
